            <ArticleTitle>The fetal origins of adult hypertension.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S39-44</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To elucidate the fetal origins of hypertension.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A systematic search was made for records of early growth, comprising measurements taken either at birth and/or during infancy, for groups of men and women now in middle-late life. Over 25,000 men and women born before 1931 were successfully traced.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Retarded growth in fetal life was strongly related to high blood pressure in adult life.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Hypertension is programmed by an adverse environment in utero. It is linked to impaired development in both early and late gestation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
